<section id="A2" class="ltx_appendix">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_appendix"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_appendix">Appendix B </span>Technical details of Recursive Feature Machines (RFMs)</h2>

<div id="A2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">RFMs are an iterative algorithm for learning features with general machine learning models by learning representations through the Average Gradient Outer Product (AGOP) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib11" title="" class="ltx_ref">Radhakrishnan et al., 2024</a>)</cite>. RFMs are initialized with a base prediction algorithm, training inputs and labels, and validation inputs and labels. RFMs fit the training data through <math id="A2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math> iterations of the following steps. RFM (1) first fits the base algorithm to the training data, (2) computes the AGOP of the fit algorithm from step 1, (3) transforms the data with the AGOP matrix, then (4) returns to step 1. The iteration proceeds for <math id="A2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math> steps, each step constructing a separate predictor. The best predictor among the iterations is selected on the validation set. In our case, as is typically done, we perform the RFM update with Kernel Ridge Regression (KRR) as the base model. We briefly explain the KRR model.</p>
</div>
<section id="A2.SS0.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">KRR</h5>

<div id="A2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="A2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X\in\mathbb{R}^{n\times d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X\in\mathbb{R}^{n\times d}</annotation></semantics></math> denote training samples with <math id="A2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{x^{(i)}}^{T}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mmultiscripts><mi>x</mi><mrow></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow></mrow><mi>T</mi></mmultiscripts><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{x^{(i)}}^{T}</annotation></semantics></math> denoting the sample in the <math id="A2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math><sup id="A2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_sup">th</sup> row of <math id="A2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> for <math id="A2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i\in[n]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i\in[n]</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y\in\mathbb{R}^{n\times c}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y\in\mathbb{R}^{n\times c}</annotation></semantics></math> denote training labels, where <math id="A2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>c</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c</annotation></semantics></math> is the number of output channels (e.g. one-hot encoded classes for <math id="A2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c&gt;2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c&gt;2</annotation></semantics></math> classes). In our case, the inputs are themselves embeddings obtained from the internal activations of parent models such as CLIP or ResNet. Let <math id="A2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K:\mathbb{R}^{d}\times\mathbb{R}^{d}\to\mathbb{R}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>d</mi></msup><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>d</mi></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>ℝ</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K:\mathbb{R}^{d}\times\mathbb{R}^{d}\to\mathbb{R}</annotation></semantics></math> denote a kernel function (a positive semi-definite, symmetric function), such as the generalized Laplacian kernel we deploy in DANCE. Given a ridge regularization parameter <math id="A2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda\geq 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda\geq 0</annotation></semantics></math>, KRR solved on the data <math id="A2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(X,y)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(X,y)</annotation></semantics></math> gives a predictor, <math id="A2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{f}:\mathbb{R}^{d}\to\mathbb{R}^{c}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>f</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>d</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>c</mi></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{f}:\mathbb{R}^{d}\to\mathbb{R}^{c}</annotation></semantics></math>, of the form:</p>
<table id="A2.EGx1" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="A2.E8"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A2.E8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\hat{f}(x)=K(x,X)\beta~," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>f</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>β</mi></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0.330em">,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\hat{f}(x)=K(x,X)\beta~,</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(8)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="A2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>β</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta</annotation></semantics></math> is the solution to the following linear system:</p>
<table id="A2.EGx2" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="A2.E9"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A2.E9.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\qquad(K(X,X)+\lambda I)\beta=y~." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>I</mi></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>β</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0.330em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\qquad(K(X,X)+\lambda I)\beta=y~.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(9)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.3" class="ltx_p">Here the notation <math id="A2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K(x,X)\in\mathbb{R}^{1\times n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K(x,X)\in\mathbb{R}^{1\times n}</annotation></semantics></math> is the <math id="A2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math>-dimensional row vector with <math id="A2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K(x,X)_{i}=K(x,x^{(i)})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>x</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K(x,X)_{i}=K(x,x^{(i)})</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K(X,X)\in\mathbb{R}^{n\times n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K(X,X)\in\mathbb{R}^{n\times n}</annotation></semantics></math> denotes the kernel matrix of pair-wise kernel evaluations <math id="A2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K(X,X)_{ij}=K(x^{(i)},x^{(j)})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>x</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>x</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K(X,X)_{ij}=K(x^{(i)},x^{(j)})</annotation></semantics></math>. The advantage of kernel functions in the context of this work is that the predictor admits a closed form solution, which can be robustly computed and generally has fast training times for datasets under <math id="A2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="70" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>70</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">70</annotation></semantics></math>k samples.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A2.SS0.SSS0.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">AGOP</h5>

<div id="A2.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The <math id="A2.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math> matrix learned by RFM is computed as the Average Gradient Outer Product (AGOP) of a KRR model. For a given trained predictor <math id="A2.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g:\mathbb{R}^{d}\rightarrow\mathbb{R}^{c}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>d</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>c</mi></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g:\mathbb{R}^{d}\rightarrow\mathbb{R}^{c}</annotation></semantics></math> and its training inputs <math id="A2.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{1},\ldots,x_{n}\in\mathbb{R}^{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>d</mi></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{1},\ldots,x_{n}\in\mathbb{R}^{d}</annotation></semantics></math>, the AGOP operator produces a matrix</p>
<table id="A2.Ex8" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A2.Ex8.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\mathrm{AGOP}(g,\{x_{i}\}_{i=1}^{n})=\frac{1}{n}\sum_{i=1}^{n}\nabla g(x_{i})\nabla g(x_{i})^{\top}\in\mathbb{R}^{d\times d}~," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>AGOP</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>n</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>n</mi></mfrac><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>n</mi></munderover><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><mi>g</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><mi>g</mi><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>⊤</mo></msup><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></msup><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{AGOP}(g,\{x_{i}\}_{i=1}^{n})=\frac{1}{n}\sum_{i=1}^{n}\nabla g(x_{i})\nabla g(x_{i})^{\top}\in\mathbb{R}^{d\times d}~,</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A2.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="A2.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\nabla g\in\mathbb{R}^{d\times c}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\nabla g\in\mathbb{R}^{d\times c}</annotation></semantics></math> is the (transposed) input-output Jacobian of the function <math id="A2.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>g</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g</annotation></semantics></math>. The AGOP matrix of a predictor captures the critical features in input space that most influence the prediction function, provably and empirically improving downstream prediction performance when used as a feature extractor <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhu et al., 2025</a>; <a href="#bib.bib7" title="" class="ltx_ref">Beaglehole et al., 2025</a>; <a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">Radhakrishnan et al., 2025</a>)</cite>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A2.SS0.SSS0.Px3" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">RFMs</h5>

<div id="A2.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We outline the particular RFM algorithm used in our work below. Given that the procedure is the same for any block, we simplify notation by omitting the block subscripts <math id="A2.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ell" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ell</annotation></semantics></math>. For any <math id="A2.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x,z\in\mathbb{R}^{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>z</mi></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>k</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x,z\in\mathbb{R}^{k}</annotation></semantics></math>, choice of <span id="A2.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">bandwidth</span> parameter <math id="A2.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math>, and a positive semi-definite, symmetric matrix <math id="A2.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M\in\mathbb{R}^{k\times k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M\in\mathbb{R}^{k\times k}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A2.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K_{M}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>K</mi><mi>M</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K_{M}</annotation></semantics></math> Mahalanobis kernel function, let <math id="A2.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K_{M}(Z,z)\in\mathbb{R}^{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>K</mi><mi>M</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Z</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>z</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>n</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K_{M}(Z,z)\in\mathbb{R}^{n}</annotation></semantics></math> such that <math id="A2.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K_{M}(Z,z)_{i}=K_{M}(a^{(i)},z)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>K</mi><mi>M</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Z</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>z</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>K</mi><mi>M</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>a</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>,</mo><mi>z</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K_{M}(Z,z)_{i}=K_{M}(a^{(i)},z)</annotation></semantics></math> and let <math id="A2.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K_{M}(Z,Z)\in\mathbb{R}^{n\times n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>K</mi><mi>M</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Z</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>Z</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K_{M}(Z,Z)\in\mathbb{R}^{n\times n}</annotation></semantics></math> such that <math id="A2.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K_{M}(Z,Z)_{i,j}=K_{M}(a^{(i)},a^{(j)})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>K</mi><mi>M</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Z</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>Z</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>K</mi><mi>M</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>a</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>a</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K_{M}(Z,Z)_{i,j}=K_{M}(a^{(i)},a^{(j)})</annotation></semantics></math>. Letting <math id="A2.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M_{0}=I" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>M</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mi>I</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M_{0}=I</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A2.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda\geq 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda\geq 0</annotation></semantics></math> denote a regularization parameter, RFM repeats the following two steps for <math id="A2.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math> iterations:</p>
<table id="A2.EGx3" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="A2.Ex9"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A2.Ex9.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\displaystyle\text{Step 1:}~~\hat{f}_{t}(z)=\beta_{t}K_{M_{t}}(Z,z)~\text{where}~\beta_{t}=y[K_{M_{t}}(Z,Z)+\lambda I]^{-1}~," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mtext>Step 1:</mtext><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>f</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>t</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>z</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>β</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><msub><mi>K</mi><msub><mi>M</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Z</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>z</mi><mo rspace="0.330em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mtext>where</mtext><msub><mi>β</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mi>y</mi><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msub><mi>K</mi><msub><mi>M</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Z</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>Z</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>+</mo><mi>λ</mi><mi>I</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\text{Step 1:}~~\hat{f}_{t}(z)=\beta_{t}K_{M_{t}}(Z,z)~\text{where}~\beta_{t}=y[K_{M_{t}}(Z,Z)+\lambda I]^{-1}~,</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(Kernel ridge regression)</span></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="A2.Ex10"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A2.Ex10.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\displaystyle\text{Step 2:}~~M_{t+1}=\frac{1}{n}\sum_{i=1}^{n}\nabla_{z}\hat{f}_{t}(a^{(i)})\nabla_{z}\hat{f}_{t}(a^{(i)})^{\top}~;" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mtext>Step 2:</mtext><msub><mi>M</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>n</mi></mfrac></mstyle><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>n</mi></munderover></mstyle><msub><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><mi>z</mi></msub><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>f</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>t</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>a</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><msub><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><mi>z</mi></msub><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>f</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>t</mi></msub><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>a</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>⊤</mo></msup><mo>;</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\text{Step 2:}~~M_{t+1}=\frac{1}{n}\sum_{i=1}^{n}\nabla_{z}\hat{f}_{t}(a^{(i)})\nabla_{z}\hat{f}_{t}(a^{(i)})^{\top}~;</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(AGOP)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A2.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="A2.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\nabla_{z}\hat{f}_{t}(a^{(i)})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mo>∇</mo><mi>z</mi></msub><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>f</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>a</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\nabla_{z}\hat{f}_{t}(a^{(i)})</annotation></semantics></math> denotes the gradient of <math id="A2.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{f}_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>f</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{f}_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> with respect to the input <math id="A2.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="z" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>z</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">z</annotation></semantics></math> at the point <math id="A2.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a^{(i)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>a</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a^{(i)}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="A2.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">We utilize the xRFM repo for the RFM implementation <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib7" title="" class="ltx_ref">Beaglehole et al., 2025</a>)</cite></p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="A3" class="ltx_appendix">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_appendix"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_appendix">Appendix C </span>Proof of Theorems</h2>

<section id="A3.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">C.1 </span>Proof of Theorem <a href="#S3.Thmtheorem1" title="Theorem 3.1. ‣ 3.1 𝑘-NN Set Nonconformity ‣ 3 Doubly Adaptive Neighborhood Conformal Estimation ‣ DANCE: Doubly Adaptive Neighborhood Conformal Estimation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.1</span></a></h3>

<div id="A3.SS1.2" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="A3.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A3.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A3.SS1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text">Recall the definition of set-valued <math id="A3.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>NN classifier:</span></p>
<table id="A3.Ex11" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A3.Ex11.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{{L}}_{k}(Z)={\rm Unique}\{\tilde{y}\in\mathcal{Y}\mid\tilde{y}\in\mathcal{N}_{k}(Z,\tilde{\mathbf{Z}})\}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>L</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>k</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Z</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>Unique</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>y</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒴</mi></mrow><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>y</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Z</mi><mo>,</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐙</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{{L}}_{k}(Z)={\rm Unique}\{\tilde{y}\in\mathcal{Y}\mid\tilde{y}\in\mathcal{N}_{k}(Z,\tilde{\mathbf{Z}})\},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A3.SS1.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="A3.SS1.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text">where the neighborhood is searched over a reference dataset, <math id="A3.SS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{D}_{\rm ref}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mi>ref</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{D}_{\rm ref}</annotation></semantics></math>, that is independent of
<math id="A3.SS1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{\mathcal{D}_{\rm cal},(Z_{n+1},y_{n+1})\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mi>cal</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>Z</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{\mathcal{D}_{\rm cal},(Z_{n+1},y_{n+1})\}</annotation></semantics></math>. Here, we add a bit of a technical tool. Assume the classifier model returns the set of prediction sets,</span></p>
<table id="A3.Ex12" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A3.Ex12.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f(x)=\{\hat{L}_{1}(Z),...,\hat{L}_{m_{knn}}(Z)\}\cup\{\hat{L}_{\infty}(Z)\}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>L</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Z</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>L</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><msub><mi>m</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Z</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mo>∪</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>L</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Z</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f(x)=\{\hat{L}_{1}(Z),...,\hat{L}_{m_{knn}}(Z)\}\cup\{\hat{L}_{\infty}(Z)\},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A3.SS1.p1.3" class="ltx_p"><span id="A3.SS1.p1.3.1" class="ltx_text">where <math id="A3.SS1.p1.m4" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\hat{L}_{\infty}(Z)=\{1,..,c\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>L</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Z</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.0835em">.</mo><mo lspace="0.0835em" rspace="0.167em">.</mo><mo>,</mo><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{L}_{\infty}(Z)=\{1,..,c\}</annotation></semantics></math>, the set of all labels. This function is available in the setting of the theorem, since it assumes the availability of <math id="A3.SS1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{L}_{k}(Z)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>L</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>k</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Z</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{L}_{k}(Z)</annotation></semantics></math> for all <math id="A3.SS1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k\in[m_{knn}]\cup\{\infty\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msub><mi>m</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo>∪</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k\in[m_{knn}]\cup\{\infty\}</annotation></semantics></math>. Then the nonconformity score function measures the quality of this <math id="A3.SS1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f(Z)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Z</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f(Z)</annotation></semantics></math> by finding the minimum <math id="A3.SS1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> that achieves <math id="A3.SS1.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y\in\hat{L}_{k}(Z)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>L</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>k</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Z</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y\in\hat{L}_{k}(Z)</annotation></semantics></math>. For feature-label pairings <math id="A3.SS1.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(Z,y)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Z</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(Z,y)</annotation></semantics></math>, we then define the rank-based nonconformity score that is implicitly defined on <math id="A3.SS1.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f(Z)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Z</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f(Z)</annotation></semantics></math> as</span></p>
<table id="A3.Ex13" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A3.Ex13.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S_{knn}(Z,y)=\min\big\{k\in[m_{knn}]\cup\{\infty\}:y\in\hat{L}_{k}(Z)\big\}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>S</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Z</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>min</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msub><mi>m</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo>∪</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi><mo rspace="0.278em" stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>L</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>k</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Z</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S_{knn}(Z,y)=\min\big\{k\in[m_{knn}]\cup\{\infty\}:y\in\hat{L}_{k}(Z)\big\},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A3.SS1.p1.4" class="ltx_p"><span id="A3.SS1.p1.4.1" class="ltx_text">where <math id="A3.SS1.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{L}_{\infty}(Z)=\mathcal{Y}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>L</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Z</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒴</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{L}_{\infty}(Z)=\mathcal{Y}</annotation></semantics></math> is the set of all labels. Since the neighborhood search is based on an independent reference dataset, not the calibration dataset, the function <math id="A3.SS1.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>f</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f</annotation></semantics></math> is not associated with the calibration and test datasets, which does not negatively affect the assumed exchangeability. Then, we define the conformal threshold <math id="A3.SS1.p1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q_{knn}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q_{knn}</annotation></semantics></math> by taking the <math id="A3.SS1.p1.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lceil(1-\alpha_{knn})(1+n)\rceil^{\text{th}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⌈</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>α</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⌉</mo></mrow><mtext>th</mtext></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lceil(1-\alpha_{knn})(1+n)\rceil^{\text{th}}</annotation></semantics></math> smallest score of the calibration scores on <math id="A3.SS1.p1.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{D}_{\rm cal}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mi>cal</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{D}_{\rm cal}</annotation></semantics></math> for some error rate <math id="A3.SS1.p1.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha_{knn}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>α</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha_{knn}</annotation></semantics></math>. By the standard split conformal prediction argument, we have</span></p>
<table id="A3.E10" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A3.E10.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1-\alpha\leq\mathbb{P}(S_{knn}(Z_{n+1},y_{n+1})\leq q_{knn})\leq 1-\alpha+\frac{1}{n+1}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>α</mi></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mi>ℙ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>S</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>Z</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>α</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mfrac></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1-\alpha\leq\mathbb{P}(S_{knn}(Z_{n+1},y_{n+1})\leq q_{knn})\leq 1-\alpha+\frac{1}{n+1},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(10)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A3.SS1.p1.5" class="ltx_p"><span id="A3.SS1.p1.5.1" class="ltx_text">where the upper bound establishes when there is no tie among the nonconformity scores of the calibration dataset and the test data point.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="A3.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A3.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A3.SS1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text">The core part in the proof is an interesting observation of inclusion monotonicity that for <math id="A3.SS1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{y}\in\mathcal{Y}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>y</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒴</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{y}\in\mathcal{Y}</annotation></semantics></math>,</span></p>
<table id="A3.E11" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A3.E11.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{y}\not\in\hat{L}_{k}(Z)\Leftrightarrow\tilde{y}\not\in\hat{L}_{j}(Z)~(\forall j\leq k)." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>y</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo>∉</mo><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>L</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>k</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Z</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⇔</mo><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>y</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo>∉</mo><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>L</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>j</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Z</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0.330em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">∀</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{y}\not\in\hat{L}_{k}(Z)\Leftrightarrow\tilde{y}\not\in\hat{L}_{j}(Z)~(\forall j\leq k).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(11)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A3.SS1.p2.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="A3.SS1.p2.2.1" class="ltx_text">Therefore, <em id="A3.SS1.p2.2.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">given <math id="A3.SS1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q_{knn}&lt;\infty" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&lt;</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q_{knn}&lt;\infty</annotation></semantics></math>,</em> there is equivalence among events such as</span></p>
<table id="A3.Ex14" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A3.Ex14.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\{S_{knn}(Z_{n+1},y_{n+1})&gt;q_{knn}\}=\cap_{k=1}^{q_{knn}}\{y_{n+1}\not\in\hat{L}_{k}(Z_{n+1})\}=\{Z_{n+1}\not\in\hat{L}_{q_{knn}}(Z_{n+1})\}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>S</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>Z</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>&gt;</mo><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0em">=</mo><msubsup><mo lspace="0em">∩</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>∉</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>L</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>k</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>Z</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>Z</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>∉</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>L</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>Z</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{S_{knn}(Z_{n+1},y_{n+1})&gt;q_{knn}\}=\cap_{k=1}^{q_{knn}}\{y_{n+1}\not\in\hat{L}_{k}(Z_{n+1})\}=\{Z_{n+1}\not\in\hat{L}_{q_{knn}}(Z_{n+1})\},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A3.SS1.p2.3" class="ltx_p"><span id="A3.SS1.p2.3.1" class="ltx_text">where the last equality is by (<a href="#A3.E11" title="Equation 11 ‣ Proof. ‣ C.1 Proof of Theorem ‣ Appendix C Proof of Theorems ‣ DANCE: Doubly Adaptive Neighborhood Conformal Estimation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">11</span></a>).
Therefore, we have <math id="A3.SS1.p2.m3" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\{S_{knn}(Z_{n+1},y_{n+1})\leq q_{knn}\}=\{y_{n+1}\in\hat{L}_{q_{knn}}(Z_{n+1})\}." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>S</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>Z</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>≤</mo><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>∈</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>L</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>Z</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{S_{knn}(Z_{n+1},y_{n+1})\leq q_{knn}\}=\{y_{n+1}\in\hat{L}_{q_{knn}}(Z_{n+1})\}.</annotation></semantics></math>
Recall the definition of the prediction set <math id="A3.SS1.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{C}_{knn}(Z)=\hat{L}_{q_{knn}}(Z)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>C</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Z</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>L</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Z</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{C}_{knn}(Z)=\hat{L}_{q_{knn}}(Z)</annotation></semantics></math>. Therefore, the bound in (<a href="#A3.E10" title="Equation 10 ‣ Proof. ‣ C.1 Proof of Theorem ‣ Appendix C Proof of Theorems ‣ DANCE: Doubly Adaptive Neighborhood Conformal Estimation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">10</span></a>) transfers to</span></p>
<table id="A3.Ex15" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A3.Ex15.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1-\alpha\leq\mathbb{P}(y_{n+1}\in\hat{C}_{knn}(Z_{n+1}))\leq 1-\alpha+\frac{1}{n+1}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>α</mi></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mi>ℙ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>y</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>C</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>Z</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>α</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mfrac></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1-\alpha\leq\mathbb{P}(y_{n+1}\in\hat{C}_{knn}(Z_{n+1}))\leq 1-\alpha+\frac{1}{n+1}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A3.SS1.p2.4" class="ltx_p"><span id="A3.SS1.p2.4.1" class="ltx_text">∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A3.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">C.2 </span>Proof of Proposition 1:</h3>

<div id="A3.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A3.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A3.SS2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Proof:</span> It follows from Boole’s inequality that:</p>
<table id="A3.EGx4" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="A3.Ex16"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A3.Ex16.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\mathbb{P}(y_{n+1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ℙ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo fence="true" lspace="0em">CLOSE</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\mathbb{P}(y_{n+1}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A3.Ex16.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\not\in\hat{C}_{knn}(Z_{n+1})\cup y_{n+1}\not\in\hat{C}_{clr}(Z_{n+1}))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo fence="true" rspace="0em">OPEN</mo><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>∉</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>C</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>Z</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∪</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><mo>∉</mo><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>C</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>l</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>Z</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\not\in\hat{C}_{knn}(Z_{n+1})\cup y_{n+1}\not\in\hat{C}_{clr}(Z_{n+1}))</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="A3.Ex17"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A3.Ex17.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\leq\mathbb{P}(y_{n+1}\not\in\hat{C}_{knn}(Z_{n+1}))+\mathbb{P}(y_{n+1}\not\in\hat{C}_{clr}(Z_{n+1}))\leq\alpha_{knn}+\alpha_{clr}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>ℙ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>y</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>∉</mo><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>C</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>Z</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>ℙ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>y</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>∉</mo><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>C</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>l</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>Z</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><msub><mi>α</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>α</mi><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>l</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\leq\mathbb{P}(y_{n+1}\not\in\hat{C}_{knn}(Z_{n+1}))+\mathbb{P}(y_{n+1}\not\in\hat{C}_{clr}(Z_{n+1}))\leq\alpha_{knn}+\alpha_{clr}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="A3.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A3.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">where bound <math id="A3.SS2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{P}(y\not\in\hat{C}_{knn}(Z_{n+1}))\leq\alpha_{knn}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>ℙ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>∉</mo><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>C</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>Z</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><msub><mi>α</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{P}(y\not\in\hat{C}_{knn}(Z_{n+1}))\leq\alpha_{knn}</annotation></semantics></math> follows from <a href="#S3.Thmtheorem1" title="Theorem 3.1. ‣ 3.1 𝑘-NN Set Nonconformity ‣ 3 Doubly Adaptive Neighborhood Conformal Estimation ‣ DANCE: Doubly Adaptive Neighborhood Conformal Estimation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.1</span></a> and split conformal bound <math id="A3.SS2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{P}(y\not\in\hat{C}_{clr}(Z_{n+1}))\leq\alpha_{clr}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>ℙ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>∉</mo><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>C</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>l</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>Z</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><msub><mi>α</mi><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>l</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{P}(y\not\in\hat{C}_{clr}(Z_{n+1}))\leq\alpha_{clr}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div class="ltx_pagination ltx_role_newpage"></div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="A4" class="ltx_appendix">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_appendix"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_appendix">Appendix D </span>Disjoint Reference Ablation Study</h2>

<div id="A4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In the main text, we used a leave-one-out strategy where the calibration set was both reused as the reference set and the set used to search for <math id="A4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>λ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda</annotation></semantics></math> to maximize data efficiency. In this section, we validate those results against a theoretically valid disjoint split. More specifically, we define the reference set <math id="A4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{D}_{\rm ref}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mi>ref</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{D}_{\rm ref}</annotation></semantics></math> as the training set <math id="A4.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{D}_{\rm sup}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mi>sup</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{D}_{\rm sup}</annotation></semantics></math> used to fit the RFM adapter. Furthermore, we evenly split the available calibration data, <math id="A4.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{D}_{\rm cal}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mi>cal</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{D}_{\rm cal}</annotation></semantics></math>, into two disjoint subsets: <math id="A4.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{D}_{\lambda}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mi>λ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{D}_{\lambda}</annotation></semantics></math>, used to select the mixing parameter <math id="A4.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>λ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="A4.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{D}_{\rm Thr}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mi>Thr</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{D}_{\rm Thr}</annotation></semantics></math>, used to compute the conformal thresholds <math id="A4.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(q_{knn},q_{clr})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>l</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(q_{knn},q_{clr})</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="A4.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A4.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Uncertainty Quantification (UQ) quality is compared between the reuse and disjoint strategies through set size, marginal coverage and class-conditional coverage violation (CCV). We evaluate with <math id="A4.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m_{knn}=100" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>m</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>100</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m_{knn}=100</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="A4.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m_{clr}=50" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>m</mi><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>l</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>50</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m_{clr}=50</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="A4.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda\in[0,0.1,...,0.9,1.0]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>0.1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>0.9</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1.0</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda\in[0,0.1,...,0.9,1.0]</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A4.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha\in[0.1,0.05]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>0.1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>0.05</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha\in[0.1,0.05]</annotation></semantics></math> on the ImageNet, ImageNet-Rendition and ImageNet-Sketch datasets.</p>
</div>
<div id="A4.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A4.p3.1" class="ltx_p">The results in Table <a href="#A4.T3" title="Table 3 ‣ Appendix D Disjoint Reference Ablation Study ‣ DANCE: Doubly Adaptive Neighborhood Conformal Estimation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a> show that the two splits are very consistent to each other between both <math id="A4.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>α</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha</annotation></semantics></math> levels in all three datasets. The disjoint split attains slightly lower CCV in all settings, indicating better conditional robustness than the reuse split. The set size efficiency difference for both splits is similar across all settings. While theoretical validity is important, we believe the close results here indicate the heuristic reuse split can be used to maximize data efficiency while maintaining the desired theoretical coverage properties DANCE provides.</p>
</div>
