<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="A5.F4.5" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Figure 4</span>: </span><span id="A5.F4.6" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Additional ablation studies. <span id="A5.F4.6.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Top Row:</span> Impact of neighbor number for <math id="A5.F4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m_{knn}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>m</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m_{knn}</annotation></semantics></math>. <span id="A5.F4.6.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Bottom Row:</span> Impact of neighbor number for <math id="A5.F4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m_{clr}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>m</mi><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>l</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m_{clr}</annotation></semantics></math></span></figcaption>
</figure>
<section id="A5.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">E.1 </span><math id="A5.SS1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m_{knn}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>m</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m_{knn}</annotation></semantics></math> Sensitivity</h3>

<div id="A5.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A5.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We find that performance of <math id="A5.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\hat{C}_{knn}(.)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>C</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">.</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{C}_{knn}(.)</annotation></semantics></math> is generally resistant to choice of <math id="A5.SS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m_{knn}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>m</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m_{knn}</annotation></semantics></math>. We search through <math id="A5.SS1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m_{knn}\in[15,25,50,100]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>m</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>15</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>25</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>50</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>100</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m_{knn}\in[15,25,50,100]</annotation></semantics></math>. Figure <a href="#A5.F4" title="Figure 4 ‣ Appendix E Neighbor Ablation Studies ‣ DANCE: Doubly Adaptive Neighborhood Conformal Estimation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a> (top row) shows the effect of <math id="A5.SS1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m_{knn}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>m</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m_{knn}</annotation></semantics></math> on the UQ metrics of coverage, set size and CCV for CLIP ViT-B/16 at <math id="A5.SS1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha=0.05" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0.05</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha=0.05</annotation></semantics></math>. As <math id="A5.SS1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m_{knn}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>m</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m_{knn}</annotation></semantics></math> increases, the set size remains steady after around <math id="A5.SS1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m_{knn}=40" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>m</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>40</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m_{knn}=40</annotation></semantics></math> with CCV also remaining steady at just under 5.8. The plots show that for very small neighbor values, the coverage lowers with the minimal marginal coverage drops below the desired 0.95, although this quickly returns to the desired nominal level. This ablation indicates limited benefit of large <math id="A5.SS1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m_{knn}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>m</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m_{knn}</annotation></semantics></math>, with it only adding computational burden.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A5.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">E.2 </span><math id="A5.SS2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m_{clr}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>m</mi><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>l</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m_{clr}</annotation></semantics></math> Sensitivity</h3>

<div id="A5.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A5.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Figure <a href="#A5.F4" title="Figure 4 ‣ Appendix E Neighbor Ablation Studies ‣ DANCE: Doubly Adaptive Neighborhood Conformal Estimation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a> (bottom row) shows the effect of <math id="A5.SS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m_{clr}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>m</mi><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>l</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m_{clr}</annotation></semantics></math> on the UQ metrics of coverage, set size and CCV for CLIP ViT-B/16 at <math id="A5.SS2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha=0.05" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0.05</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha=0.05</annotation></semantics></math>. We search through <math id="A5.SS2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m_{clr}\in[15,25,50,100]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>m</mi><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>l</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow></msub><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>15</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>25</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>50</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>100</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m_{clr}\in[15,25,50,100]</annotation></semantics></math>. As for <math id="A5.SS2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m_{knn}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>m</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m_{knn}</annotation></semantics></math>, for very small neighbor sizes, the coverage drops below the desired 0.95 level, however this again quickly corrects. From the second plot, we see the choice of <math id="A5.SS2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m_{clr}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>m</mi><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>l</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m_{clr}</annotation></semantics></math> highly impacts the resulting set-size where as <math id="A5.SS2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m_{clr}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>m</mi><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>l</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m_{clr}</annotation></semantics></math> increases, the set size increases as well. We see the opposite for CCV, where there is a sharp drop in CCV until 50 neighbors. We see that as we go from <math id="A5.SS2.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m_{clr}=50" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>m</mi><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>l</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>50</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m_{clr}=50</annotation></semantics></math> to <math id="A5.SS2.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="100" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>100</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">100</annotation></semantics></math>, the set size continues to increase; however, the CCV no longer drops, indicating the addition of more neighbors also brings limited benefits.</p>
</div>
<div class="ltx_pagination ltx_role_newpage"></div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="A6" class="ltx_appendix">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_appendix"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_appendix">Appendix F </span>Selection of <math id="A6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>λ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda</annotation></semantics></math></h2>

<div id="A6.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A6.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The hyperparameter <math id="A6.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>λ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda</annotation></semantics></math> controls the allocation of <math id="A6.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>α</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha</annotation></semantics></math> between <math id="A6.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S_{knn}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>S</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S_{knn}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A6.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S_{clr}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>S</mi><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>l</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S_{clr}</annotation></semantics></math>. We determine the optimal <math id="A6.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>λ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda</annotation></semantics></math> through a grid search over <math id="A6.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[0,0.1,...,0.9,1.0]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>0.1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>0.9</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1.0</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[0,0.1,...,0.9,1.0]</annotation></semantics></math>. First <math id="A6.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{D}_{\rm cal}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mi>cal</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{D}_{\rm cal}</annotation></semantics></math> is partitioned into reference set <math id="A6.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{D}_{\rm ref}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mi>ref</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{D}_{\rm ref}</annotation></semantics></math> (80%) and tuning set <math id="A6.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{D}_{\rm val}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mi>val</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{D}_{\rm val}</annotation></semantics></math> (20%). For each candidate <math id="A6.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>λ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda</annotation></semantics></math>, we calibrate on <math id="A6.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{D}_{\rm ref}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mi>ref</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{D}_{\rm ref}</annotation></semantics></math> using leave-one-out neighbor search before evaluating prediction sets for <math id="A6.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{D}_{\rm Test}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mi>Test</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{D}_{\rm Test}</annotation></semantics></math>. We select the parameter that minimizes a weighted combination of the standardized set size (<math id="A6.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Size(\lambda)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>z</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Size(\lambda)</annotation></semantics></math>), and class-conditional coverage violation (<math id="A6.p1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="CCV(\lambda)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>C</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>V</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">CCV(\lambda)</annotation></semantics></math>) such that</p>
</div>
<div id="A6.p2" class="ltx_para">
<table id="A6.Ex18" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A6.Ex18.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda_{\text{opt}}=\underset{\lambda}{{\mathop{\rm arg\,min}}}(0.8\cdot Size(\lambda)+0.2\cdot CCV(\lambda))" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>λ</mi><mtext>opt</mtext></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><munder accentunder="true"><mrow><mi>arg</mi><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>min</mi></mrow><mo mathsize="0.700em">𝜆</mo></munder><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mn>0.8</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>z</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>0.2</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mi>C</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>C</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>V</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda_{\text{opt}}=\underset{\lambda}{{\mathop{\rm arg\,min}}}(0.8\cdot Size(\lambda)+0.2\cdot CCV(\lambda))</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="A6.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A6.p3.1" class="ltx_p">where standardization is computed relative to mean and standard deviation across all candidates. This weighting ensures <math id="A6.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>λ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda</annotation></semantics></math> is selected prioritizing set size efficiency, while still retaining a level of conditional robustness.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A7" class="ltx_appendix">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_appendix"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_appendix">Appendix G </span>Detailed Results</h2>

<section id="A7.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">G.1 </span>RFM and Kernel Parameter Fit Time</h3>

<div id="A7.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A7.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Figure <a href="#A7.F5" title="Figure 5 ‣ G.1 RFM and Kernel Parameter Fit Time ‣ Appendix G Detailed Results ‣ DANCE: Doubly Adaptive Neighborhood Conformal Estimation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a> shows the computational scalability of the RFM methodology. We notice a linear trend on the <math id="A7.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\log" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>log</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\log</annotation></semantics></math>-<math id="A7.SS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\log" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>log</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\log</annotation></semantics></math> scale, showing a predictable timing quality with respect to data volume. This efficiency is substantial, where the longest fit time for ImageNet required only 713 seconds (<math id="A7.SS1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sim" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>∼</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sim</annotation></semantics></math> 12 minutes), with the majority of datasets requiring under 60 seconds to achieve high predictive performance. We also note that this fit time is for 25 Bayesian Optimization evaluations to fit <span id="A7.SS1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_markedasmath ltx_font_bold">M</span>, <math id="A7.SS1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{\beta}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝜷</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{\beta}</annotation></semantics></math>, and determine optimal <math id="A7.SS1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\xi,L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ξ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>L</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\xi,L</annotation></semantics></math>. This demonstrates that RFM is a computationally efficient solution for task-adaption and kernel fitting.</p>
</div>
<div id="A7.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A7.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">The BayesianOptimization package <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib56" title="" class="ltx_ref">Nogueira, 2014</a>)</cite> was used for parameter optimization.</p>
</div>
<figure id="A7.F5" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2602.20652v1/Plots/sizeVsTime.png" id="A7.F5.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:286/188;" width="286" height="188" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="A7.F5.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Figure 5</span>: </span><span id="A7.F5.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">RFM Training Set Size vs Training Time (Both axes are log-scaled.)</span></figcaption>
</figure>
</section>
<section id="A7.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">G.2 </span>ViT-B/16 Results</h3>

<div id="A7.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A7.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The following show detailed results for CLIP ViT-B/16. Best and second best set size and CCV are <span id="A7.SS2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">bolded</span> and <span id="A7.SS2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_underline">underlined</span>, respectively.</p>
</div>
<figure id="A7.T4" class="ltx_table">
<div id="A7.T4.3" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_align_center ltx_transformed_outer" style="width:276.0pt;height:426.2pt;vertical-align:-211.7pt;"><span class="ltx_transformed_inner" style="transform:translate(-76.9pt,118.7pt) scale(0.642239430898192,0.642239430898192) ;">
